; `.default` on a head that is not a rust PRIMITIVE the default's value class fits is refused at the
; `.default` APPLICATION — both parsing routes — instead of aborting inside `RustType::default`. The
; head here is a REFUSED prelude name, which makes this the closure-breach shape: `new_type` has
; already recorded the graceful refusal for `undefined` and returned its inert `Fixed(Null)`
; placeholder, so an abort one step later would DESTROY a rejection `finalize` was going to report.
; Both refusals now reach the drain, which is why this row is `error (graceful)`.
; The refusal is not exclusive to refused names — a SUPPORTED prelude name with no rust primitive
; refuses identically (`tests/robustness/ctl_default_supported_head.cddl`), which is the reason the
; check sits at the application and not at any name seam.
x = undefined .default 1
